# Edge hardening + merchant-profile routing — 2026-07-26
#
# APPLIED LIVE on the VPS 2026-07-26 (unlike the PREPARE-ONLY snippets beside it).
# Backups taken before the change:
#   /root/nginx-backups-20260726/tilla.gudman.xyz.conf.PRE-FIX
#   /root/nginx-backups-20260726/nginx.conf.PRE-FIX
# `nginx -t` was run before every reload; all sibling sites on the shared box
# (warden / verdikt / bequest / gapguard / reef / shop) re-verified 200 after.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. FIX: /m/{address} merchant profiles were 404 in public
#
# `sitemap.xml` emits one `/m/{address}` link per merchant with a public live
# store (app/main.py, the `merchant_profile` handler answers them), but the vhost
# had no `location /m/`, so every one of those URLs fell through to
# `location / { root /opt/tilla/www; }` and returned the static 404. The app was
# serving them correctly on 127.0.0.1:8040 the whole time — this was purely an
# edge-routing gap, and it meant the sitemap advertised four dead URLs to
# crawlers. Added immediately above the static `location /`:
#
#     location /m/ { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8040; proxy_set_header Host $host; proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https; }
#
# Prefix (not exact) match, because the address is part of the path. It sits
# above `location /` but cannot shadow `location /s/` — different prefix.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Security headers at server level
#
# The vhost previously sent only X-Content-Type-Options and Referrer-Policy, and
# sent them WITHOUT `always`, so they were dropped on error responses. There was
# no HSTS anywhere and no X-Frame-Options outside `location /s/` — meaning the
# dashboard, the page that drives wallet `personal_sign` sign-in and refund
# actions, was framable by any origin. Server block now sends:
#
#     add_header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff always;
#     add_header Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin always;
#     add_header X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN always;
#     add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300" always;
#     add_header Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()" always;
#
# HSTS max-age is deliberately SHORT (300s). HSTS is a durable browser-side
# commitment: a long max-age means a client refuses plain HTTP to this host for
# that whole period, so a lapsed certificate becomes an outage rather than a
# warning. 300s was chosen so the header is present and correct for review
# without betting the site on cert renewal. RAISE IT to 31536000 (plus
# `includeSubDomains; preload` if every subdomain is HTTPS) once the renewal
# cron has been observed succeeding at least once unattended.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Header inheritance inside `location /s/`
#
# GOTCHA worth remembering: nginx `add_header` does NOT merge across levels — a
# single `add_header` inside a location cancels ALL server-level ones. Because
# `location /s/` declared its own two, store pages were silently missing
# Referrer-Policy. Every header is now restated inside that block. Any future
# location that adds a header must restate the full set too.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. gzip for JSON/JS (/etc/nginx/nginx.conf, not the vhost)
#
# `gzip on;` was set but `gzip_types` stayed commented out, and nginx's default
# is `text/html` ONLY — so every JSON feed, the discovery API, and embed.js
# shipped uncompressed. Uncommented and widened:
#
#     gzip_types text/plain text/css application/json application/javascript text/javascript application/xml text/xml image/svg+xml;
#
# Measured effect: GET /discovery/resources 16782 -> 2877 bytes on the wire.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Removed 9 stale vhost copies from sites-enabled
#
# `nginx.conf` does `include /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/*` — an unfiltered glob, so
# it was loading 9 `tilla.gudman.xyz.conf.bak-*` / `.bak.preproduct` files as real
# server blocks. `nginx -t` printed a `conflicting server name` warning for each,
# and the live config won only because `.conf` sorts before `.bak-*`. A rename or
# a differently-sorting filename could silently have served `bak-m4`, which
# predates /dashboard, /api/merchant and the rate limits. All nine moved to
# /root/nginx-backups-20260726/; `nginx -t` is now free of conflicting-name
# warnings. Keep vhost backups OUTSIDE sites-enabled.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. limit_req on the write endpoints and /mcp — APPLIED
#
# The `tilla_create` zone (12r/m) was DEFINED in conf.d/tilla-limit-req.conf and
# referenced NOWHERE, so the three paid write endpoints ran unthrottled — and
# /create-store both spends an LLM call and writes a store to disk. /mcp was
# likewise open. Now:
#
#     location = /create-store   limit_req zone=tilla_create burst=5  nodelay;
#     location = /upgrade-store  limit_req zone=tilla_create burst=5  nodelay;
#     location = /add-product    limit_req zone=tilla_create burst=5  nodelay;
#     location = /mcp            limit_req zone=tilla_api    burst=20 nodelay;
#
# (/api/ and the checkout_sessions regex were already on tilla_api.)
# Backup before this step: tilla.gudman.xyz.conf.PRE-RATELIMIT.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT DONE — deliberately left for after the hackathon
#
# * CSP: the pages use inline <style>/<script> throughout, so a meaningful CSP
#   needs nonces or hashes threaded through the generator. Not a same-day change.
# * limit_req on /feeds/ and the store-action regex (buy|mcp|reviews|quote|…).
#   Read-only or already-priced paths, so lower urgency than the writes above.
# * Cache-Control on static HTML and og.png (ETag/Last-Modified only today).
# * A real /favicon.ico (pages use an inline SVG icon, so tabs render; only
#   direct /favicon.ico fetches by old crawlers 404).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT A DEFECT, checked and recorded so nobody re-opens it
#
# * `https://<custom-domain>/s/{slug}/` is a 404, and that is correct. Store
#   HTML is served by nginx from /opt/tilla/stores/ on the PLATFORM host only;
#   the app has no /s/{slug}/ HTML route. A custom domain serves its store at
#   its ROOT (`custom_domain_root`), which is what the canonical URL points at.
#   Only the app-routed children (/s/{slug}/feed.json etc.) answer under /s/
#   there, because those live on the agentic router.
